# General Debate on the First Supplementary Estimates for 1968–69
Tamil title: 1968-69ஆம் ஆண்டிற்கான முதலாவது துணை நிதி நிலை அறிக்கைமீது பொது விவாதம்

Speech 48 — Date: 19 August 1968

## Image 1

### General Debate on the First Supplementary Estimates for 1968–69

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Mr Chairman, it gives me great pleasure that all the honourable members who spoke generally about these first supplementary estimates found no fault with the purposes for which we have stated that these funds are required. Questions of basic policy are not usually raised during a debate on supplementary estimates. There is no rule that they must not be raised, but ordinarily they are not. The proper purpose of this debate should be to examine whether the purposes for which these supplementary estimates are presented are indeed necessary and whether they deserve acceptance. In keeping with that purpose, I first thank everyone who participated in today's debate for finding no fault and for stating that all these items are necessary.

Our Deputy Leader of the Opposition, Thiru Ponnappa Nadar, said that although he accepted the necessity of increasing the dearness allowance paid to government employees by more than three crore rupees, it should be merged with their salary and an effort should be made to that end. This is a view that has generally been raised and pressed by many people. There is no need to wonder what connection it has with the supplementary demands for grants.

## Image 2

It is a matter requiring general consideration. He referred to the view that a “Pay Commission” should be appointed for this purpose. Various sections in our country have been pressing this view. It is one of the many questions being considered by the Union and State Governments. For now, however, we recognised that it was fair and necessary to raise the dearness allowance of our government employees to the level paid to Union Government employees, and we have done so. Members know that some other States hesitated to grant such an increase and that disturbances arose as a result. I believe many will be pleased that, without any such dispute, this Government acted so as to provide its employees with immediate relief in this just matter.

Although supplementary estimates exceeding six crore rupees have been sought, almost half that sum goes towards payments to our government employees. The rest is for agricultural development and for our lift-irrigation schemes: the Government must take over private electricity-extension schemes and spend a portion of this money to increase lift irrigation through them. One member remarked that, as though adding further prosperity to already prosperous regions, as much as sixty-six lakh rupees had been allotted to the Thanjavur district. Why should this amount be spent on the Thanjavur region? This does not mean that facilities should not be provided to every other region. Experts pointed out that the drainage portions of the Kallanai system in Thanjavur had remained unattended for a very long time. On their advice, a special scheme was therefore undertaken.

## Image 3

The scheme has an estimated cost of sixty-six lakh rupees, and the Government has decided to spend twenty-six lakh rupees from these estimates for it. I believe no one will deny that it is necessary.

Similarly, these estimates state that an additional allocation of fifty lakh rupees should be made to provide loans to cultivators for purchasing fertiliser. I believe members will be pleased that, in all, approximately two crore rupees have been allotted for this purpose.

As Thiru Ponnappa Nadar noted, this Government allotted eighty-eight lakh rupees for a drinking-water scheme serving the large industrial complex being established at Manali, and no one will deny that the Government undertook that scheme. Indeed, our Ponnappa Nadar urged that it be accelerated. I am very pleased to report that it is being accelerated and that the work is moving rapidly towards completion.

Honourable members also spoke about what must be done for people returning from Ceylon. The allotment of ten lakh rupees here should not be taken to mean that this entire undertaking can be completed with ten lakh rupees. Because some had understood it that way, they said that the returnees should be given land in the hill forests, places in tea estates, and settlements in the Nilgiris. All these proposals have been developed into several schemes, sent to the Government of India, approved by it, and brought into operation today. This ten-lakh-rupee sum is only the amount allotted to provide loan assistance if those returning from Ceylon wish to establish some small industry.

## Image 4

Apart from this, members would be greatly pleased if they learned what assistance our Government is providing to the returnees from Ceylon. Government announcements about it are issued from time to time. Senior officials visited the Andaman Islands and identified ten or fifteen thousand acres of good, fertile land. They recommended to the Union Government that it be granted to the returnees for cultivation, and the Union Government considered and accepted the recommendation. A scheme to settle them in tea estates in the Gudalur region is likewise in operation. There is a similar scheme in the Nilgiris. In every district, lands suitable for cultivation are being surveyed, and arrangements are being made to engage capable cultivators returning from Ceylon on those lands.

If we learn the complete details, friends will understand that the ten-lakh-rupee item in the list is only one particular among the measures undertaken and not the whole account.

Similarly, a larger grant than has hitherto been allotted has been provided to schools and technical schools. I believe the House will welcome the allocation of 66-2/3 per cent to schools and 75 per cent to polytechnics.

Only two matters drew dissatisfaction from some members. They asked whether prizes should be awarded to motion pictures and whether it was just to allot one lakh rupees for producing the newsreel. As they say in English, “It all depends upon the taste.” It depends on each person's likes and dislikes. When we consider that prizes exceeding one lakh rupees are being awarded to good-quality films—

## Image 5

—we must also take into account the revenue the Government derives from the cinema industry. We receive six or seven crore rupees in entertainment tax. If they had realised that the release of good films would draw larger audiences and thereby bring in as much as another crore of rupees in revenue corresponding to what we spend, they would not have questioned it.

**Thiru N. S. V. Chithan:** The Chief Minister said while speaking that it would bring in considerable revenue. If that is so, are they considering lifting prohibition because doing so would produce considerable revenue?

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** It was people holding views like his who lifted prohibition in Congress-ruled States. My point was not merely that it produces revenue; I also said that quality would rise. Congress members will immediately say that better-quality liquor than what existed before could be produced, and that this would bring in still more revenue.

**Thiru K. Vinayakam:** Sir, is our Hon. Chief Minister in order or is it right on his part to criticise the policy of a neighbouring State irrespective of the policy pursued by our State or can we interfere like this on the floor of this House? Does it amount to good taste?

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Talks about 'taste' should to reciprocal and not unilateral. It is in order for the hon. Member to suggest that I should lift Prohibition and earn money? It is not in good taste.

**Thiru K. Vinayakam:** The question before the House is, is he right in criticising the policies pursued by a neighbouring State. Usually we do not interfere in such matters and that is the convention that we have developed. So, is he right in saying so?

---NOTES---
- The English interventions on Image 5 are reproduced exactly as printed in the source, including the apparent grammatical error “should to reciprocal.” The surrounding Tamil has been translated normally; the source quotation has not been silently emended.
- “பஞ்சப்படி” is rendered as “dearness allowance,” its administrative meaning in this debate.
- “இறைவை நீர்ப்பாசனம்” is rendered as “lift irrigation.”
- “தமாஷா வரி” is rendered as “entertainment tax,” the historical fiscal term for cinema and similar amusements.
